  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Battery Basics · Last reviewed 2026-09-17</p>
      <h1>Battery Capacity: Ah, Wh, CCA and RC Explained</h1>
      <p class="lead">Capacity is the tank — and the tank has two units, two extra ratings, and one honest adjustment most labels leave to the fine print.</p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>Ah</strong> = how many amps for how many hours at the rated rate; <strong>Wh</strong> = Ah × voltage (the voltage-independent unit); <strong>CCA</strong> = the cold-cranking punch; <strong>RC</strong> = minutes at 25A. And the <strong>usable</strong> capacity is the nameplate times the depth-of-discharge window — ~50% for lead-acid, 80–90% for LFP.
      </div>

      <h2>What does amp-hours mean?</h2>
      <p>A 100 Ah battery delivers <strong>10A for about 10 hours</strong> — or 5A for about 20 — at its rated (C20) rate and before usable-depth adjustments. The fine print matters: the nameplate Ah assumes the slow 20-hour discharge, and heavy loads deliver less (the <a href="/lithium/c-rate/">Peukert effect</a>). Ah compares batteries only at the same voltage — which is why Wh exists.</p>

      <h2>What is the difference between Ah and Wh?</h2>
      <p>Wh removes the voltage ambiguity: <strong>Wh = Ah × V</strong>. 100 Ah at 12V is 1,200 Wh; 100 Ah at 48V is 4,800 Wh — same amp-hours, four times the energy. Use Ah within one voltage class; use Wh to compare across them (see <a href="/technology/ah-vs-wh/">the full comparison</a>).</p>

      <h2>What are CCA and RC?</h2>
      <p>The two automotive extras: <strong>CCA</strong> (cold cranking amps) is the current the battery can deliver for 30 seconds at −18°C — the starting punch, standard-dependent (see <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">the CCA ladder</a>). <strong>RC</strong> (reserve capacity) is the minutes at 25A before falling to 10.5V — the "engine off, lights on" endurance, roughly 1.5–1.7× the Ah rating in minutes (see <a href="/lead-acid/reserve-capacity/">reserve capacity</a>).</p>

      <h2>Why is the usable capacity smaller than the label?</h2>
      <p>Because the chemistry's longevity window is smaller than its tank: lead-acid lives longest cycled to <strong>~50%</strong> of its nameplate; LFP to <strong>80–90%</strong>. The label states the tank; the depth-of-discharge states how much of the tank you should spend (see <a href="/lithium/lifespan/">cycle-life numbers</a>). The <a href="/tools/runtime-calculator/">runtime calculator</a> encodes the difference directly.</p>

      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My position:</strong> Capacity is the battery market's most misread number — quoted without its rate, its voltage or its usable depth — and all three omissions run in the same direction: making the battery look bigger than it is.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why:</strong> The three qualifiers are the difference between a plan and a wish. A capacity number that carries its rate, voltage and depth is a usable engineering input; one that does not is advertising. This lesson exists so readers can tell the two apart at a glance.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What does amp-hours mean on a battery?</h3>
      <p>Amp-hours (Ah) measure capacity: a 100 Ah battery delivers 10 amps for about 10 hours at its rated 20-hour rate. The rating assumes that slow rate — heavy loads deliver less, the Peukert effect — and the usable amount is further reduced by the depth-of-discharge window.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between Ah and Wh?</h3>
      <p>Wh = Ah × voltage, which makes Wh the voltage-independent energy unit. 100 Ah is 1,200 Wh at 12V but 4,800 Wh at 48V. Compare Ah within one voltage class and Wh across them.</p>

      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the Ah rating quoted at the C20 rate, or a slower rate that flatters the number?</li>
        <li>Should the comparison be in Wh instead, because the batteries run at different voltages?</li>
        <li>What usable depth does the application require — roughly 50% for lead-acid, 80–90% for LFP?</li>
        <li>Does the job need CCA (starting) or reserve capacity (engine-off endurance), not just stored Ah?</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>Read any capacity number with its rate, voltage and usable depth attached — the label states the tank, and the depth-of-discharge window states how much of it you may actually spend. A capacity figure missing those three qualifiers is advertising, not an engineering input.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from the engineering references cited on the linked pages.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery engineering references — rate capacity and Peukert behaviour.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer datasheets — Ah, CCA and RC ratings.</li>
      </ul>

